475 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 226 0 R
/E (Medical reports constitute the primary medium through which diagnostic and prognostic information is communicated from healthcare providers to patients. Despite their critical role, these reports are authored in specialized clinical language characterized by dense biomedical terminology, abbreviated test names, quantitative values without contextual interpretation, and implicit diagnostic reasoning. This creates a fundamental communication barrier: patients receive documents containing vital health information yet lack the linguistic and medical knowledge required to interpret them. The problem is compounded by the volume and variety of reports generated across pathology, radiology, cardiology, and other specialties, each with domain-specific conventions.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
